Google, to no ones surprise, has become
one of the biggest economic forces in the
world: it's how you find stuff to buy, new
business partners, and so forth.

Like anything connected with money, it
has attracted swarms of scam artists.

One kind of scam is artificial ways to
push your web page up in Google's
rankings. I get spammed with ads for such
"services" several times a day, among the
hundreds of spam emails I receive (average
perhaps 400 per day).

The only "ethical" way for a web page to
be ranked high on Google is because lots
of people like the page's contents enough
to link to it on one of their own web pages
to make it easier for readers of their own
pages to find it.

The more evocative words you put in the
link you create, the easier it will be for
others to write web searches that match
the words you used. In my case, I just
called my link "NetHack Mastermind"
which seemed to cover the subject, but
"NetHack Mastermind for Solving the
Drawbridge Melody Puzzle in the Game
of NetHack" would get hits from more
different search strings, like, say,
"Drawbridge Puzzle", which mentions
neither NetHack nor Mastermind.

